# G130 — Turtle Graphics

*v2. The milestone finale. See `turtle.md` for prose: integer-degree heading,
integer-scaled sin/cos table, program-as-data + stateful interpreter,
state stack for nested procedures, vector-line canvas.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Turtle Garden

[turtle-garden @save-path
    IN ORDER TO
        draw a recursive fractal tree using the turtle's state stack
        and export it as an SVG vault artifact

    @canvas <- [tl[new-canvas]]

    [tl[execute {canvas: @canvas,
                  program: @[
                      [tl[turn {deg: 90}]]
                      ...[branch 5 100]
                  ]}]]

    @svg <- [tl[export-svg {canvas: @canvas}]]
    [vault[save {path: @save-path, content: @svg}]]
]

[branch @depth @length
    IN ORDER TO
        draw a trunk segment of @length, then recurse two branches
        each half-length at ±30°, preserving the trunk position via the stack

    @depth
        where greater than 0
        IN ORDER TO continue recursion (otherwise emit nothing)

    [
        [tl[forward {n: @length}]]
        [tl[push-state]]
        [tl[turn {deg: -30}]]
        ...[branch (@depth - 1) (@length / 2)]
        [tl[pop-state]]
        [tl[push-state]]
        [tl[turn {deg: 30}]]
        ...[branch (@depth - 1) (@length / 2)]
        [tl[pop-state]]
    ]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[tl
    sin-table       {angle}          ;; integer × 10000
    cos-table       {angle}
    new-canvas
    execute         {canvas, program}
    step            {canvas, command}
    forward         {n}               ;; command constructor
    backward        {n}
    turn            {deg}
    set-heading     {heading}
    pen-up
    pen-down
    set-color       {color}
    set-pen-width   {width}
    push-state
    pop-state
    goto            {x, y}
    render          {canvas, width, height, bg}
    export-svg      {canvas}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: program-as-data is native

A turtle program is an ordered bundle of commissions. The `execute`
verb folds over them, each step mutating the canvas:

@program <- [
    [tl[forward {n: 100}]]
    [tl[turn {deg: 90}]]
    [tl[forward {n: 100}]]
    [tl[turn {deg: 90}]]
    [tl[forward {n: 100}]]
    [tl[turn {deg: 90}]]
    [tl[forward {n: 100}]]
    [tl[turn {deg: 90}]]
]
[tl[execute {canvas: @c, program: @program}]]

This *is* the Innate idiom. Commissions are values; lists of
commissions are programs. The v1 `Vec<Command>` is exactly this
structure — Innate had it natively all along.

---

## Integer-scaled trig as cross-resolver contract

[tl[sin-table {angle: @a}
    where
        a is wrapped to 0..=359 first via ((a mod 360) + 360) mod 360
        and the result is integer, scaled by 10000
        and sin-table(0) = 0, sin-table(90) = 10000, sin-table(45) = 7071
        and the table is byte-identical across all conforming resolvers
]

[tl[cos-table {angle: @a}
    where cos(a) = sin((a + 90) mod 360)
]

The cross-language byte-identity contract is surfaced as a `where`
clause. Any resolver claiming to implement `tl` must satisfy it.

---

## State stack preserves turtle identity, not lines

[tl[push-state]
    where snapshots (x, y, heading, pen-down, pen-color, pen-width)
        but does NOT snapshot the canvas line list
        so lines emitted between push and pop remain in the canvas
        while the turtle returns to its pre-push position
]

The v1 insight — "branch lines preserved; only turtle identity restores"
— lives in this `where` clause.

---

## Axis-aligned closure is exact; off-axis has bounded drift

[tl[execute {canvas: @c, program: [forward 100, turn 90] × 4}
    where turtle returns to exactly (0, 0) heading 0
        because cos/sin at 0°/90°/180°/270° are ±10000 or 0 exactly
]

[tl[execute {canvas: @c, program: [turn 45, forward 100, turn 90] × 4}
    where turtle returns to within ±2 pixels of (0, 0)
        because cos(45) = sin(45) = 7071
        and 100 × 7071 / 10000 = 70 (integer division loses 0.71 per step)
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [tl[sin-table {angle: 0}]]      ;; -> 0
    -> [tl[cos-table {angle: 0}]]      ;; -> 10000
    -> [tl[sin-table {angle: 45}]]     ;; -> 7071 (= cos 45)
    -> [tl[sin-table {angle: 90}]]     ;; -> 10000
    -> [tl[sin-table {angle: 270}]]    ;; -> -10000

    @c <- [tl[new-canvas]]
    @square <- [
        [tl[forward {n: 100}]] [tl[turn {deg: 90}]]
        [tl[forward {n: 100}]] [tl[turn {deg: 90}]]
        [tl[forward {n: 100}]] [tl[turn {deg: 90}]]
        [tl[forward {n: 100}]] [tl[turn {deg: 90}]]
    ]
    [tl[execute {canvas: @c, program: @square}]]

    -> @c:turtle:x, @c:turtle:y           ;; -> 0, 0 (exact)
    -> @c:turtle:heading                   ;; -> 0
    -> @c:lines:count                      ;; -> 4

    ;; State stack: branch off, come back
    @c2 <- [tl[new-canvas]]
    [tl[execute {canvas: @c2, program: [
        [tl[forward {n: 50}]]
        [tl[push-state]]
        [tl[turn {deg: 90}]] [tl[forward {n: 30}]]
        [tl[pop-state]]
    ]}]]
    -> @c2:turtle:x, @c2:turtle:y          ;; -> 50, 0 (back to pre-push)
    -> @c2:turtle:heading                   ;; -> 0
    -> @c2:lines:count                      ;; -> 2 (trunk + branch drawn)
]
